# Cell Cycle and Stem Cells
#
# The cyclin-CDK oscillator and its checkpoints as a state machine, then pluripotency, reprogramming, and morphogen gradients that decide what a dividing cell becomes.

#>cell_biology_cell_cycle
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!cell_biology
#:form #!state_machine
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Cell Cycle and Stem Cells"
 ~#claim: "Rising and falling cyclin-CDK activity drives a cell from growth to copying to division, checkpoints hold it until damage is fixed and every chromosome is attached, and stem cells keep other fates open."
 ~#proof: "Cyclin of Hunt, cdc2 of Nurse, checkpoints of Hartwell and Weinert, the unattached-kinetochore signal of Rieder, and the reprogramming factors of Takahashi and Yamanaka."
 ~#door: "Walk the cycle states, read each checkpoint as a guard on one transition, then turn to potency and gradients."
 ~#audience: "advanced students who know replication and signaling"
 continuity: "Cell Cycle and Stem Cells | every chromosome is attached"
 cold_open: "A cell will not split its chromosomes until every chromosome is attached, and one loose kinetochore is enough to make it wait."
}

